<BaseLayout 
  title="30-Year Career Retrospective & Temporal HUD // Jesse Fulton"
  description="Reverse-chronological career retrospective combining first-person narrative memoir with a sleek interactive temporal HUD rail spanning 30 years of roles, 6 US patents, Cannes Lions, and verified builds."
>
  <Header />

  <main class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-6 py-12 md:py-16">
    <!-- Top Hero Section -->
    <header class="border-b border-charcoal/80 pb-10 mb-8">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
        <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-gold animate-pulse"></span>
        <span class="font-mono text-xs text-gold uppercase tracking-widest font-semibold">
          // CAREER RETROSPECTIVE // 30-YEAR EVOLUTION // 1995 – PRESENT
        </span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-6xl font-display font-bold tracking-tight mb-4 heading-glow-gold">
        The Unpaved Road
      </h1>
      <p class="text-titanium-muted max-w-3xl text-base md:text-lg font-light leading-relaxed">
        My career has never been a sterile corporate ladder. It has been a thirty-year collision between radical creative curiosity and unforgiving engineering realities—starting on physical breadboards at Disney, freezing in unheated warehouses writing GPU shaders with Scott Snibbe, drilling enclosures at 3 AM in Rio during the Olympics, and running 72 liquid-cooled supercomputers off-grid in Iowa.
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Sticky Act Jump Navigation Bar -->
    <nav class="sticky top-[69px] z-40 bg-obsidian/95 backdrop-blur-md border-y border-charcoal/80 py-3.5 mb-16 -mx-6 px-6 flex items-center justify-between gap-4 overflow-x-auto">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 min-w-max">
        <span class="font-mono text-[11px] text-titanium-muted uppercase tracking-wider hidden sm:inline mr-2">
          JUMP TO ERA:
        </span>
        <a 
          href="#act-1" 
          class="font-mono text-xs px-3 py-1.5 rounded bg-charcoal/40 border border-charcoal text-cyan hover:border-cyan hover:bg-cyan/10 transition-all font-semibold"
        >
          [ ACT I // 2022–NOW ]
        </a>
        <a 
          href="#act-2" 
          class="font-mono text-xs px-3 py-1.5 rounded bg-charcoal/40 border border-charcoal text-gold hover:border-gold hover:bg-gold/10 transition-all font-semibold"
        >
          [ ACT II // 2018–2022 ]
        </a>
        <a 
          href="#act-3" 
          class="font-mono text-xs px-3 py-1.5 rounded bg-charcoal/40 border border-charcoal text-magenta hover:border-magenta hover:bg-magenta/10 transition-all font-semibold"
        >
          [ ACT III // 2014–2018 ]
        </a>
        <a 
          href="#act-4" 
          class="font-mono text-xs px-3 py-1.5 rounded bg-charcoal/40 border border-charcoal text-gold hover:border-gold hover:bg-gold/10 transition-all font-semibold"
        >
          [ ACT IV // 2010–2014 ]
        </a>
        <a 
          href="#act-5" 
          class="font-mono text-xs px-3 py-1.5 rounded bg-charcoal/40 border border-charcoal text-lime hover:border-lime hover:bg-lime/10 transition-all font-semibold"
        >
          [ ACT V // 1995–2010 ]
        </a>
      </div>

      <a 
        href="/portfolio" 
        class="font-mono text-xs px-3 py-1.5 rounded border border-charcoal bg-obsidian text-titanium hover:border-gold hover:text-gold transition-colors min-w-max hidden md:block"
      >
        FULL 20-PROJECT CATALOG →
      </a>
    </nav>

    <!-- Master 5-Act Narrative & Interactive HUD Streams -->
    <div class="space-y-28 md:space-y-36">

      {acts.map((act) => (
        <section id={act.id} class="scroll-mt-36 border-t border-charcoal pt-10">
          
          <!-- Act Header Banner -->
          <div class="flex flex-col md:flex-row md:items-end justify-between gap-4 pb-8 mb-10 border-b border-charcoal/60">
            <div>
              <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2">
                <span class={`w-2.5 h-2.5 rounded-full ${act.dotBg} animate-pulse`}></span>
                <span class={`font-mono text-xs font-bold ${act.themeColor === 'cyan' ? 'text-cyan' : (act.themeColor === 'magenta' ? 'text-magenta' : (act.themeColor === 'lime' ? 'text-lime' : 'text-gold'))} uppercase tracking-widest`}>
                  {act.actNum} // {act.period} // {act.title}
                </span>
              </div>
              <h2 class={`text-3xl md:text-4xl font-display font-bold text-titanium mt-0 mb-2 ${act.glowClass}`}>
                {act.subtitle}
              </h2>
            </div>

            <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
              {act.focusPills.map(pill => (
                <span class={`font-mono text-[10px] px-2.5 py-1 rounded border uppercase font-bold ${act.badgeClass}`}>
                  {pill}
                </span>
              ))}
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- 2-Column Grid: Narrative Left (7 Cols) + Sleek Temporal HUD Rail Right (5 Cols) -->
          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-12 gap-10 lg:gap-14 items-start">
            
            <!-- LEFT COLUMN: Gonzo Narrative Story (Strictly First-Person) -->
            <div class="lg:col-span-7 space-y-6 text-base text-titanium/90 font-light leading-relaxed">
              
              {act.id === 'act-1' && (
                <>
                  <p>
                    We start at the bleeding edge. While public tech discourse remains caught up in consumer chatbot wrappers and vanity benchmarks, the actual engineering crucible is where compute physics collide head-on with deterministic agent runtimes.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    At <a href="/portfolio/cato-green-ai" class="text-gold underline font-medium">Cato Digital</a>, as Chief of Staff to the CEO, we set out to break the hyperscaler monopoly on compute by commercializing circular second-life silicon. In our most intense operational sprint, our team racked and unified <strong>72 liquid-cooled NVIDIA DGX-2 supercomputers</strong> in an off-grid Iowa datacenter in under 24 hours. We built custom power-distribution SLAs, re-engineered cooling matrices, and developed data-driven forecasting models that increased company valuation by $25M+ following back-to-back down-rounds.
                  </p>
                  
                  <div class="punk-panel p-6 bg-obsidian border-l-4 border-l-cyan my-6">
                    <div class="font-mono text-xs text-cyan uppercase tracking-wider font-bold mb-2">
                      // CRITICAL FIELD NOTE: DETERMINISTIC AGENT EVALS
                    </div>
                    <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-titanium-muted leading-relaxed font-light mb-0">
                      "In healthcare, a hallucination isn't a glitch—it's a clinical violation. When an AI agent conducts patient intake on chronic sleep or metabolic risk, auditors demand zero-tolerance compliance and step-by-step reasoning inspection. If you can't prove why an agent made a clinical inference, it has no business in production."
                    </p>
                  </div>

                  <p>
                    At <a href="/portfolio/thrive-ai-health" class="text-cyan underline font-medium">Thrive AI Health</a>, I stepped into exactly that arena as Principal Product Engineer. There were no established playbooks for deploying autonomous generative agents into clinical behavior change. I architected deterministic agent orchestration runtimes, context compilation pipelines, and synthetic conversation simulators. Building automated regression test harnesses and HIPAA audit controls gave our engineering team the confidence to accelerate developer velocity by 5x while reducing infrastructure costs by 40%.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    Beyond operational builds, I regularly advise enterprise executives, sovereign AI initiatives, and high-velocity founders—bringing thirty years of multidisciplinary breadth and deep technical spikes to separate durable systems architecture from vendor slideware.
                  </p>
                </>
              )}

              {act.id === 'act-2' && (
                <>
                  <p>
                    If the first decade of my career was about proving I could build anything with code, hardware, and raw persistence, this chapter was about an equally critical discipline: technical brilliance is useless if the business model is broken or organizational politics paralyze execution.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    At Code & Theory, I directed product management and engineering for <a href="/portfolio/pwc-enterprise-alignment" class="text-gold underline font-medium">PwC Sightline</a> ($50B+ corporate tax practice). Senior equity partners spoke exclusively in spreadsheets, had seen multiple design agencies flame out, and harbored deep skepticism toward agency jargon. Rather than presenting polished static mockups, I designed an iterative 9-month research and prototype roadshow across 9 multibillion-dollar business units—conducting 100+ partner interviews and stress-testing 50+ rapid prototypes. We turned a modest $60k discovery pilot into the agency's #1 account by revenue, growing revenue 20x in six months and securing three years of additional project funding.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    At Coinbase, following the acquisition of Bison Trails, I served as Product Manager for Developer Platform, tasked with transforming $30B+ in institutional staking infrastructure into a self-serve Web3 developer ecosystem. I standardized API primitives and developer workflows across 25+ distinct blockchains while deploying customizable telemetry dashboards across 60,000 nodes deployed on 32 blockchain protocols.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    To formalize twenty years of operational gut intuition with institutional rigor, I completed my Master of Science in Innovation & Entrepreneurship (MSIE) at <a href="/portfolio/hec-paris-venture-design" class="text-gold underline font-medium">HEC Paris</a>. Anchoring my engineering background in corporate finance, unit economic modeling, and venture incubation frameworks gave me the vocabulary to bridge boardrooms and engineering trenches seamlessly.
                  </p>
                </>
              )}

              {act.id === 'act-3' && (
                <>
                  <p>
                    This was the high-wire act of global agency firefighting. When Fortune 50 clients commit millions to international launches, there is no safety net, no sandbox mode, and no "we'll patch it in sprint four."
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    At <a href="/portfolio/juxt-interactive-systems" class="text-gold underline font-medium">JUXT</a>, as Director of Technology, I directed a 20-person creative engineering SWAT team. We banned static Figma handoffs and pioneered <em>"Co-Design in Code"</em>—pairing visual designers and developers directly inside live TouchDesigner nodes, GLSL shaders, and React canvases. At Dreamforce, we delivered the <a href="/portfolio/salesforce-iq" class="text-gold underline font-medium">SalesforceIQ 6K curved speed-trivia pavilion</a> (swapping a cracked 120" display on the floor during load-in without missing the keynote). For <a href="/portfolio/sage-summit" class="text-gold underline font-medium">Sage Summit</a> in New Orleans, we engineered 30-foot interactive curved walls using VR spatial pre-visualization to calibrate sightlines before physical buildout.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    Moving to AKQA as Group Technical Director escalated the operational stakes:
                  </p>

                  <div class="space-y-4 my-6">
                    <div class="punk-panel p-6 bg-obsidian border-l-4 border-l-magenta">
                      <div class="font-mono text-xs text-magenta uppercase tracking-wider font-bold mb-1">
                        // DESTINY 2 GHOST COMPANION (BUNGIE / ACTIVISION / AMAZON)
                      </div>
                      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-titanium/90 font-light leading-relaxed mb-0">
                        We packed a custom Alexa skill into a 3D-printed Ghost, flew to Bungie HQ in Bellevue, and literally invented the rules of Voice UX for live AAA console gaming. Working around Bungie's strict combat API rate limits, we designed in-character dialog fallback state machines for Nolan North's voice lines, earning a Cannes Silver Lion, Gold Clio, Project Isaac Award, and presenting our findings at GDC 2018 on behalf of Amazon Games.
                      </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="punk-panel p-6 bg-obsidian border-l-4 border-l-cyan">
                      <div class="font-mono text-xs text-cyan uppercase tracking-wider font-bold mb-1">
                        // VISA RIOPOOL (RIO 2016 OLYMPICS / UBER)
                      </div>
                      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-titanium/90 font-light leading-relaxed mb-0">
                        We mounted real-time computer vision rigs into moving Uber cabs across Rio de Janeiro. We battled optical IR washout with specialized UV window film, tracked down a power drill at 3 AM from an Uber driver's brother in a strip mall to punch thermal holes in overheating enclosures, and rescued an unplugged router minutes before the international press arrived.
                      </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="punk-panel p-6 bg-obsidian border-l-4 border-l-lime">
                      <div class="font-mono text-xs text-lime uppercase tracking-wider font-bold mb-1">
                        // NIKE & JORDAN FLAGSHIP SPATIAL SYSTEMS
                      </div>
                      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-titanium/90 font-light leading-relaxed mb-0">
                        Built Cinder C++ 3D configurators with 1-hour in-store sneaker printing in Portland (SwooshID), and executed 3 AM red-eye Manhattan supply runs to wire interactive treadmill simulators across three NYC flagships (LunarEpic).
                      </p>
                    </div>
                  </div>

                  <p>
                    Simultaneously, for Shell and <a href="/portfolio/my-jiffy-lube" class="text-cyan underline font-medium">My Jiffy Lube</a>, we architected distributed data pipelines synchronizing 2,000+ franchise service closet servers directly to central Salesforce cloud telemetry.
                  </p>
                </>
              )}

              {act.id === 'act-4' && (
                <>
                  <p>
                    The early 2010s were the golden era of creative computing—a wild frontier where GPUs were just landing in mobile pockets and the browser was suddenly capable of hardware-accelerated 3D rendering.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    I was performing live projection-mapped VJ sets at the <a href="/portfolio/zero1-eyebeam-exhibits" class="text-gold underline font-medium">ZERO1 Biennial</a> in San Jose, exhibiting network art at Eyebeam NYC, and coding WebGL tributes like <em>Flying Toasters</em> (earning Honorable Mention at The Creators Project SF).
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    Then interactive media pioneer Scott Snibbe recruited me to <a href="/portfolio/eyegroove-video-platform" class="text-gold underline font-medium">Eyegroove</a> as Lead Back-End Engineer. Long before TikTok, Musical.ly, or Instagram Stories existed, our premise was simple: the mobile phone is an expressive, performative instrument. We set out to build real-time interactive video with GPU shaders, gesture-driven timelines, and audio-reactive effects running at 60fps directly on mobile hardware.
                  </p>

                  <div class="punk-panel p-6 bg-obsidian border-l-4 border-l-gold my-6">
                    <div class="font-mono text-xs text-gold uppercase tracking-wider font-bold mb-2">
                      // THE RUNTIME CRUCIBLE: UNHEATED WAREHOUSE SHADERS
                    </div>
                    <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-titanium-muted leading-relaxed font-light mb-0">
                      "We operated out of an unheated San Francisco warehouse where my desk was parked directly outside the bathroom door. I sat there in heavy winter coats solo-architecting the distributed cloud transcoding pipeline from scratch—asynchronous video encoding across multiple bitrates and codecs for adaptive HLS streaming, plus acoustic feature extraction for music recommendations, scaling to 40k DAUs."
                    </p>
                  </div>

                  <p>
                    Collaborating directly with Scott on mathematical interaction models resulted in <strong>6 issued US Utility Patents</strong> covering touch choreography, real-time media modification, and distributed state synchronization. In 2016, Eyegroove was acquired by Facebook (Meta), infusing our patents and shader pipelines directly into Instagram Reels and Meta AR cameras.
                  </p>
                </>
              )}

              {act.id === 'act-5' && (
                <>
                  <p>
                    Everything began with a foundational curiosity: how machines execute logic, and how the human mind interprets meaning.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    At Johns Hopkins University, I pursued Computer Science and Psychology simultaneously. That dual foundation established an invariant that has guided my entire career: computer science is only half the battle. If a system doesn't align with human mental models, cognitive limits, and perceptual attention, its architecture is essentially useless.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    At UC Santa Cruz, during my Digital Arts & New Media (DANM) graduate research fellowship, I treated code as an adversarial cultural medium. In 2009—years before the term "Filter Bubble" was coined—my <a href="/portfolio/software-as-art-thesis" class="text-lime underline font-medium">Software as Art Thesis</a> investigated algorithmic curation and attention commodification. I built <em>Saving Mary Jessica</em> (a custom CNC robotic pen plotter translating search engine bias into physical ink bleeds), <em>Captchafy</em> (rendering web text into adversarial anti-OCR images to defend human writing from automated machine scrapers), and autonomous bot takeovers.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    We bridged that research into civic science with the <a href="/portfolio/ucsc-wildlife-tracker" class="text-cyan underline font-medium">UCSC Wildlife Tracker</a>, an ESP-inspired Facebook game crowdsourcing camera-trap wildlife labels across the Santa Cruz Mountains to train early computer vision datasets for biologists.
                  </p>

